    <section class="cd-sec">
        <span class="visa-pill">The practical part</span>
        <h2>How to Book an Appointment at a Public Hospital</h2>
        <p>Public hospitals in China are high-volume, and most booking is done through their official <strong>WeChat account or app</strong> — usually in Chinese. As a foreigner, you have three realistic paths:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Hospital international department:</strong> book by phone or email — English-speaking staff, but response times vary.</li>
            <li><strong>Official WeChat account / app:</strong> self-service, but Chinese-language and often requires a local phone number.</li>
            <li><strong>Coordinator (us):</strong> we book the appointment, translate, and handle the paperwork — you just show up.</li>
        </ol>
        <h3>You'll typically need</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>A Chinese mobile number (buy a local SIM at the airport with your passport).</li>
            <li>Your passport (used as your hospital ID).</li>
            <li>Any prior imaging and reports, ideally translated.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="cd-sec">
        <h2>What to Expect on the Day</h2>
        <p>Chinese public hospitals are efficient but crowded. A typical appointment day looks like this:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Register</strong> at the outpatient desk — show your passport and pay the registration fee.</li>
            <li><strong>See the doctor</strong> — consultations are often shorter than you may be used to; bring your questions.</li>
            <li><strong>Pay for tests</strong> at the cashier (WeChat/Alipay/cash).</li>
            <li><strong>Get imaging/labs done</strong> — often same-day or next-day results.</li>
            <li><strong>Collect results</strong> and, if needed, book a follow-up or schedule treatment.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Without Chinese, this is where a coordinator or an international department makes the biggest difference.</p>
    </section>

    <h2 class="sp-section-title">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="sp-faq">
        <div class="sp-faq-item"><h3>What apps do I need as a foreigner in China?</h3><p>WeChat (messaging + payments), Alipay (payments), a translation app, a maps app (Amap or Apple Maps), and Didi for ride-hailing. Install and test a VPN before you leave.</p></div>
        <div class="sp-faq-item"><h3>How do foreigners book an appointment at a Chinese public hospital?</h3><p>Through the hospital's official WeChat account or app (usually Chinese), through the international department by phone/email, or through a coordinator who handles booking, translation, and paperwork.</p></div>
        <div class="sp-faq-item"><h3>Do I need a Chinese phone number to use hospital booking apps?</h3><p>Many booking systems require one. Buy a local SIM at the airport with your passport. Some international departments accept overseas numbers and email.</p></div>
        <div class="sp-faq-item"><h3>Can I use my home maps app in China?</h3><p>Apple Maps works in China, but Google Maps is unreliable. Use Amap or Baidu Maps locally, and a translation app for signs and menus.</p></div>
    </div>

